ge in symptoms with head/neck position adjustments", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Abnormality of the upper arm', 'Recurrent subcortical infarcts'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=25%, source=primekg]\n\nSubclavian Steal Syndrome (True Neurovascular Type) vs Thoracic Outlet Syndrome (Non-specific/Combined Type):\n  Favours first:  abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal foot morphology\n  Favours second: abnormality of the ribs, arthralgia, edema, myalgia, venous thrombosis\n\nRelated to Subclavian Steal Syndrome (True Neurovascular Type): cerebrovascular disorder, syndromic disease\n\nRelated to Thoracic Outlet Syndrome (Non-specific/Combined Type): arterial thoracic outlet syndrome, neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome, syndromic disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  today his hand felt so weak he dropped his cup of tea → hand muscle atrophy → Subclavian Steal Syndrome (True Neurovascular Type)\n  today his hand felt so weak he dropped his cup of tea → thenar muscle weakness → Subclavian Steal Syndrome (True Neurovascular Type)"}
